2. One gradient, verified by hand
==============================================================================
  first attempt, model as in Session 9: fp64 model, central difference, eps 1e-6
    backward() +0.004259130001   nudge +0.003061712661   relative error 2.81e-01  <- they should agree to ~1e-9
  they do not. The error grows as eps shrinks, even in fp64 -- the signature of lost precision.
  cause: RMSNorm computed x.float(). For bf16 that is an upcast; for an fp64 model it is a silent
  DOWNCAST to fp32 inside every norm, so the 'fp64' loss only carried fp32's 7 digits.
  fix (model.py): promote to at least fp32 -- x.to(torch.promote_types(x.dtype, torch.float32)).

  after the fix -- weight blocks.1.ffn.up.weight[5, 17], batch 2 x 32 tokens
  backward() in fp64 : +0.004259130307   <- treated as ground truth
  backward() in fp32 : +0.004259136040
  nudge  +/-1e-6 fp64 : +0.004259130826   (central difference)
  |nudge - backward|  : 5.19e-10   relative 1.22e-07   -> agree to 7 significant figures

  the same check across nudge sizes -- relative error against the fp64 backward()
       eps | fp64 central fp64 forward | fp32 central fp32 forward
     1e-01 |     7.56e-06     4.28e-03 |     7.56e-07     4.17e-03
     1e-02 |     7.55e-08     4.27e-04 |     4.42e-05     2.91e-04
     1e-03 |     7.24e-10     4.27e-05 |     1.30e-03     3.17e-03
     1e-04 |     9.44e-10     4.27e-06 |     1.61e-02     3.23e-02
     1e-05 |     3.23e-09     4.62e-07 |     1.39e-01     2.85e-01
     1e-06 |     1.22e-07     8.66e-08 |     3.16e+00     1.78e+00
     1e-07 |     3.01e-06     3.01e-06 |     3.18e+01     2.90e+01
     1e-08 |     9.51e-06     9.51e-06 |     9.14e+01     9.83e+01
  fp32 is best at eps=1e-01 (7.6e-07); smaller nudges get worse, because the loss change drops below fp32's ~7 digits and the difference is mostly rounding

